                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[u]Ambition and Premise[/u]\r\n\r\nAn ambitious indie visual novel that combines a cyberpunk corporate conspiracy, science fiction, romance simulation, and multiple-choice visual novel gameplay into something that is genuinely engaging despite its flaws. The unique and compelling premise centers on a permanently rain-soaked London in 2099, corrupt corporations, advanced nanotechnology, mysterious women, and a protagonist who finds himself drawn into events far larger than himself.\r\n\r\n[u]Strengths and Drawbacks[/u]\r\n\r\nThe highlights are excellent cyberpunk atmosphere, an interesting blend of corporate thriller and romance simulation, multiple branching routes with meaningful choices, plenty of replay value, memorable soundtrack and artwork, several genuinely satisfying character endings, and an ambitious story. The flaws include a convoluted, uneven story, abrupt transitions, several characters who are difficult to sympathize with, and English voice acting that ranges from decent to unintentionally funny.\r\n\r\n[u]Story and Narrative[/u]\r\n\r\nThe storytelling of a sci-fi thriller is rough but entertaining. The mystery surrounding Nanotech, Tanya, and the larger conspiracy was compelling, but the pacing could feel uneven at times. Certain plot points appear suddenly, while others do not receive enough development before the story moves on. The story occasionally becomes convoluted and somewhat disjointed, especially as multiple factions and character motivations begin to collide. However, it never fully reaches the point where everything becomes complete nonsense. Most plot twists have some logical foundation, even if the execution is not always smooth.\r\n\r\n[u]Cast and Characters[/u]\r\n\r\nThe cast is memorable, although not always particularly likable. Luke Black serves as the protagonist, but he is far from a perfect hero. He can come across as selfish, indecisive, frustrating, or surprisingly courageous. Some branching routes show significant character growth, while others leave him difficult to root for. Tanya Vanic is the most intriguing character. She drives much of the mystery surrounding the plot and has one of the strongest romantic storylines. While she is not always easy to understand, she is consistently interesting. Helen Hughes provides a nice contrast to Tanya and has some genuinely heartfelt moments. Her routes tend to emphasize the more emotional side of the story. Tom Sparks is another character who can be surprisingly difficult to root for. His personality and decisions can be frustrating, though he still contributes meaningfully to the overall narrative. These characters are not universally lovable, but they are flawed in ways that often fit the world.\r\n\r\n[u]Romance and Multiple Endings[/u]\r\n\r\nSome endings are intentionally tragic. Some are bittersweet. Others are surprisingly satisfying. Some of the most personally strongest endings include Luke ending up with Tanya, Luke ending up with Helen, Luke successfully building relationships with both Tanya and Helen, and Luke redeeming himself, stepping up, and ultimately becoming the hero the story needs. Those endings felt the most rewarding after everything the characters go through. To unlock these unique endings, it is best to use multiple save slots, as many choices significantly alter later events, and constantly replaying from the beginning can become tedious. Keeping saves before major decisions makes unlocking all 24 Unique Endings dramatically easier and saves dozens of hours of repeated reading. There is also a fair amount of fanservice. It never completely overwhelms the main story, but it is definitely present and worth mentioning.\r\n\r\n[u]Voice Acting[/u]\r\n\r\nThe English voice acting and performances are noteworthy but extremely inconsistent. Some scenes sound perfectly acceptable. Others are unfortunately pretty cringey, with awkward line delivery and strange emotional timing.\r\n",
